    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d530">

                <pb facs="0622" n="612" sameAs="frus:frus1943China/pg612"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.24/1660a: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to the Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Gauss</hi>)<anchor n="35" xml:id="d530-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-10-01T22:00:00-04:00">October 1, 1943—10
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1407. Shipment of Lend-Lease supplies for China by the Turk-Sib Railway and
                    overland route. For your information, Tehran reports that according to the
                    British Minister two convoys of Lend-Lease supplies consisting largely of small
                    arms and ammunition have arrived at Meshed but that entry into Russia has been
                    refused. From Calcutta Schaberg of Lend-Lease reports that the Russian
                    representatives there have indefinitely canceled arrangements to ship Chinese
                    supplies over this route on the grounds that they cannot handle movement at the
                    present time.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Moscow.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berle</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d530-f1" n="35" type="source" xml:id="d530-f1-content">
                <p>Repeated on the same date to the Ambassador in the Soviet Union as No. 938.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
